meta {
  name: Re-point a placement
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/placements/{{placementId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "content_item_id": "{{libraryItemId}}"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 422
}

docs {
  The four ends of a placement -- curriculum, curriculum node, content item and
  content node -- are fixed when it is created. The refusal says what to do
  instead: reject this one and create another.

  This is the rule that keeps a confirmation honest. Somebody confirmed that
  those pages teach that topic; if either end could be edited afterwards, the
  confirmation would stay attached to a claim nobody ever read. Rejecting and
  re-creating leaves both rows, which is the history somebody will want when
  the mapping is questioned.

  It is refused even when the new value equals the old one, as here. The check
  is on the field being sent at all, not on whether it changes anything, so a
  client cannot discover the rule only when the value happens to differ.
}
